Let  $A,B,C$  be the vertices of the right triangle with  $a=5,b=\sqrt{37},c=2\sqrt{3}$  and  $G\in BC,E\in AB,F\in AC$  be the vertices of the equilateral triangle. As (sort of) pointed out in a few earlier posts, it's well-known that the area of an inscribed equilateral triangle of the reference triangle is minimized when it is the pedal triangle of the first isodynamic point; label this point  $D.$  Since  $D$  lies on the  $A,B,C$ -Apollonian Circles, it follows that  $AD\cdot BC=BD\cdot CA=CD\cdot BA;$  thus, <details><summary>we get a system of 3 equations to bash:</summary>For simplicity, let  $a=BG=ED,b=BE=GD,$  and  $x=BD=EG=GF=FE.$  The property mentioned earlier of  $D$  gives us  $CD=\frac{BD\cdot CA}{BA}=\frac{x\sqrt{37}}{2\sqrt{3}},AD=\frac{x\sqrt{37}}{5}$  so the Pythagorean Theorem applied to  $\triangle CGD,\triangle AED,\triangle EDB$  gives us
\begin{align*}
a^2+b^2&=x^2 
a^2+(2\sqrt{3}-b)^2&=\frac{37x^2}{25} 
(5-a)^2 + b^2&=\frac{37x^2}{12}.
\end{align*}</details> Solving (which hopefully doesn't take too long; it's mostly just plugging and chugging square roots :P), we arrive at  $x=\frac{10\sqrt{3\cdot 67}}{67};$  hence, our final answer is then  $\frac{x^2\sqrt{3}}{4}=\frac{75\sqrt{3}}{67}\implies\boxed{145}.$   $\blacksquare$ ok i just realized that this is basically the dumber version of ABCDE's solution but at least i posted the isodynamic points handout!

