Next, shift  $DEF$  so that  $E$  coincides with  $C$ . Here,  $DC=CF=FD=10$  and  $$ BF_0\cdot (5\sqrt{3}-BF_0)=BD_0\cdot (5+BD_0). $$  After solving this system of equations, we can find (with some computation) that  $$ [D_0E_0F_0]=\frac{D_0F_0^2\cdot\sqrt{3}}{4}=\frac{(BD_0^2+BF_0^2)\cdot\sqrt{3}}{4}=\frac{75\sqrt{3}}{67}, $$  and thus,  $m+n+p=\boxed{145}$ .

I decided to redo this problem today, and somehow totally independently of my solution a little less than a year ago, trigonometry actually works. 

Let  $D, E, F$  be on  $\overline{BC}, \overline{AC}, \overline{AB}$  respectively such that they are the vertices of an equilateral triangle. Furthermore, let  $DF=EF=s$ , and set  $\angle BFD = \alpha$ . The key equation is   $$ AF+BF = s\left[\frac{\sin(\alpha+60^\circ-A)}{\sin A} + \cos A\right] = 2\sqrt 3 = AB, $$   so \begin{align*} s &= \frac{2\sqrt 3 \sin A}{\sin(\alpha+60^\circ - A) + \cos \alpha \sin A} 
&= \frac{2\sqrt 3 \cdot \frac 5{\sqrt{37}}}{\sin \alpha \cos(60^\circ- A)+\cos \alpha \sin(60^\circ - A) + \frac{5\sqrt{37}}{37}\cos \alpha} 
&= \frac{\frac{10\sqrt{111}}{37}}{\frac{7\sqrt 3}{2\sqrt{37}} \sin \alpha + \frac {11}{2\sqrt{37}} \cos \alpha} 
&= \frac{20\sqrt 3}{7\sqrt 3 \sin \alpha + 6 \cos \alpha} 
&\geq \frac{20\sqrt 3}{\sqrt{268}} = \frac{10\sqrt 3}{67}.
\end{align*}
So the area is greater than or equal to  $\frac{300}{67} \cdot \frac{\sqrt 3}4 = \frac{75\sqrt 3}{67}$ . Extract  $\boxed{145}$ .**<span style="color:purple">Remark.**</span> Our choice of  $\alpha$  along  $\overline{AB}$  doesn't matter -- we can obtain the same optimizations with respect to  $x$  (albeit with the  $\sin$  and  $\cos$  terms switched). This shows the magic of the independent variable in such problems.

A solution that uses trig instead of coordinates (which is what most people in this thread used)

<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $x$  be the rightmost angle of the bottom left right-triangle. Then, by setting  $s$  to be the side length of the equilateral triangle and dropping an altitude from the vertex of the equilateral triangle that is on the hypotenuse of the large right triangle to the bottom leg of the large right triangle gives:
\begin{align*}
s &= \frac{5}{\sin(x+30)+\frac{5\sin(60+x)}{2\sqrt{3}}} 
&= \frac{5}{\frac{7\cos{x}}{4} + \frac{11\sin{x}}{4\sqrt{3}}} 
&\ge \frac{5}{\sqrt{\frac{49}{16}+\frac{121}{48}}} 
&=\frac{30}{\sqrt{201}}
\end{align*}
Where the last line follows from cauchy-schwarz. Therefore, the minimum possible area is  $\frac{\sqrt3}{4}\left(\frac{30}{\sqrt{201}}\right)^2 = \frac{75\sqrt{3}}{67},$  so the answer is  $\boxed{145}$</details>

Drop the triangle in the complex plane so that two vertices of the equilateral triangle are  $a$  and  $bi$ . Then note that the third vertex is simply a rotation of  $bi$  by  $-60$  degrees about  $a$ , which is just,
\begin{align*}
(bi - a)\left(\frac{1 - \sqrt{3}i}{2} \right) + a  = \frac{1}{2}\left[ \left(b\sqrt{3} + a\right) + i \left(b + a\sqrt{3} \right) \right]
\end{align*}
Now shifting to coordinates we want to have the third coordinate to lie on,
\begin{align*}
y = -\frac{2\sqrt{3}}{5}x + 2\sqrt{3} = 2\sqrt{3} \left(1 - \frac{x}{5} \right)
\end{align*}
and so we require,
\begin{align*}
b + a\sqrt{3} &= 2\sqrt{3}\left(2 - \frac{a + b\sqrt{3}}{5} \right)
5b +5 a\sqrt{3} &= 2\sqrt{3} \left(10 - a - b\sqrt{3} \right)
5b + 5a\sqrt{3} &= 20\sqrt{3} - 2a\sqrt{3} - 6b
11b + 7a\sqrt{3} &= 20\sqrt{3}
\end{align*}
Thus given these conditions we wish to minimize  $a^2 + b^2$  which by Cauchy Schwarz we may do when,
\begin{align*}
(a^2 + b^2)(147 + 121) &\geq 1200
(a^2 + b^2) &\geq \frac{1200}{268} = \frac{300}{67}
\end{align*}
and hence we have a minimum area of,
\begin{align*}
\frac{300\sqrt{3}}{4 \cdot 67} = \boxed{\frac{75\sqrt{3}}{67}}
\end{align*}

Let  $a+b=t$ . Then
\begin{align*}
\sum_{a+b\le 6}\binom6a\binom6b\binom6{a+b}&=\sum_{t\le 6}\binom6t\sum_{a+b=t}\binom6a\binom6b 
&= \sum_{t\le 6}\binom 6t\binom{12}{t} = \sum_{t\le 6}\binom{6}{6-t}\binom {12}t 
&= \binom{18}{6}\equiv \boxed{564}\pmod{1000}.
\end{align*}

Note that,
\begin{align*}
a_n \equiv a_{n - 1} + n \pmod{99}
\end{align*}
so we want the smallest  $n$  such that,
\begin{align*}
10 + (11 + 12 + \dots + n) \equiv 0 \pmod{99}
\end{align*}
This is simply,
\begin{align*}
\frac{(10 + n)(n - 9)}{2} &\equiv 0 \pmod{99}
(10 + n)(n - 9) &\equiv 0 \pmod{99}
\end{align*}
Looking modulo  $11$  this implies that either  $n \equiv 1 \pmod{11}$  or  $n \equiv 9 \pmod{11}$ . Looking modulo  $9$  we find that,
\begin{align*}
(1 + n)n \equiv 0 \pmod{9}
\end{align*}
which yields  $n \equiv 0, 8 \pmod{9}$ . The smallest  $n$  satisfying both congruences is  $\boxed{45}$ .

