Solved with **GeronimoStilton**. We use the unbounded knapsack dynamic programming algorithm. The idea is to let  $m[c]$  denote the maximum number of popsicles that could be purchased at a given price  $c$ , then write the recursion  $m[c] = \max\{m[c-1] + 1, m[c-2] + 3, m[c-3] + 5\}$ . This yields the following table.
\begin{tabular}{c|c} $c$ & $m[c]$  
\hline
0&0 
\hline
1&1 
\hline
2& $\max(1+1,0+3) = 3$ \hline
3& $\max(3+1,1+3,0+5) = 5$ \hline
4& $\max(5+1,3+3,1+5) = 6$ \hline
5& $\max(6+1,5+3,3+5) = 8$ \hline
6& $\max(8+1,6+3,5+5) = 10$ \hline
7& $\max(10+1,8+3,6+5) = 11$ \hline
8& $\max(11+1,10+3,8+5) = 13$ .
\end{tabular}
