Let  $A=1$ ,  $C=x^2$ ,  $B=1/x^2$  where  $|x|=1$ . We see that  $D$  is the intersection of the chord connecting  $B$  and the midpoint of  $\widehat{AC}$ , so
\begin{align*}
d &= \frac{bx(a+c)-ac(b+x)}{bx-ac}
&=\frac{\frac{1}{x}(1+x^2)-x^2(x+1/x^2)}{1/x-x^2} 
&= \frac{x(1+x^2)-x^2(x^3+1)}{x(1-x^3)} 
&= \frac{x(1-x)(1+x^2+x^3)}{x(1-x)(1+x+x^2)} 
&= \frac{1+x^2+x^3}{1+x+x^2}.
\end{align*}
We also see
\[\overline{d}=\frac{1+x+x^3}{x(1+x+x^2)}\]
We also have that  $\overline{e}=e$  and  $\frac{e-d}{a-c}\in i\mathbb{R}$ , so
\[\frac{e-d}{1-x^2}=-\frac{e-\overline{d}}{1-1/x^2},\]
so  $e-d=x^2e-x^2\overline{d}$ , so
\begin{align*}
e(x^2-1)&=\frac{x^2(1+x+x^3)}{x(1+x+x^2)}-\frac{1+x^2+x^3}{1+x+x^2}&=\frac{x+x^4-1-x^3}{1+x+x^2}
&= \frac{(x^3+1)(x-1)}{1+x+x^2}.
\end{align*}
Thus,  $e=\frac{1-x+x^2}{1+x+x^2}$ . We also have  $I=x+1/x-1$  by incenter formula, so
\begin{align*}
I'&=a+c-ac(x+1/x-1) 
&= 1+x^2-x^2(x+1/x-1) 
&= 1-x+2x^2-x^3.
\end{align*}
Note that  $(1+x+x^2)I'=1+2x^2+x^4-x^5$ .

We want to show that  $B,D,E,I'$  cyclic. We will show
\begin{align*}
&((1+x+x^2)B-1,(1+x+x^2)D-1,(1+x+x^2)E-1,(1+x+x^2)I'-1)
=&(1/x+1/x^2,x^2+x^3,x^2-x,2x^2+x^4-x^5)=:(p,q,r,s)
\end{align*}
are cyclic, which clearly suffices. Note that
\begin{align*}
\frac{q-p}{q-r} &= \frac{x^2+x^3-1/x-1/x^2}{x^3+x} 
&= \frac{x^5 + x^4 -x -1}{x^3(1+x^2)} 
&= \frac{(x^4-1)(x+1)}{x^3(1+x^2)} 
&= \frac{(x^2-1)(x+1)}{x^3} 
&= \frac{(x-1)(x+1)^2}{x^3},
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
\frac{s-p}{s-r} &= \frac{2x^2+x^4-x^5-1/x-1/x^2}{-x^5+x^4+x^2+x} 
&= \frac{-x^7+x^6+2x^4-x-1}{x^3(1+x+x^3-x^4)} 
&= \frac{(x^2+1)(x^3-1)(1+x-x^2)}{x^3(1+x^2)(1+x-x^2)} 
&= \frac{(x^3-1)}{x^3}.
\end{align*}
Thus,
\[k:=\frac{q-p}{q-r}\div\frac{s-p}{s-r} = \frac{(x-1)(x+1)^2}{x^3}\cdot\frac{x^3}{(x-1)(x^2+x+1)}=\frac{(x+1)^2}{x^2+x+1}.\]
It suffices to show that  $k\in\mathbb{R}$ . We see that
\[\overline{k}=\frac{(1+1/x)^2}{1/x^2+1/x+1}=\frac{(1+x)^2}{1+x+x^2}=k,\]
as desired.  $\blacksquare$ Maybe I should spend this time sleeping rather than bashing :p

