<details><summary>Solution</summary>We use complex numbers. The incircle is the unit circle. Let the intouch point opposite  $A$  be 1, so that  $AI$  is the real axis. Let the intouch point opposite  $B$  be  $x$ . Since the triangle is iscoceles, the intouch point opposite  $C$  is the reflection of  $x$  over  $AI$ , the real axis, so it is  $1/x$ . 

We have  $B=\tfrac{2/x}{1/x+1} = \tfrac{2}{x+1}$ . We locate  $D$  by knowing that  $D\in XX$  and  $B,I,D$  collinear. So
\[ d+x^2 \overline{d} = 2x, \ d = \frac{2}{x+1}k,  \text{ for some } k\in \mathbb{R}. \]
Solving,
\begin{align*}
 \frac{2k}{x+1} + x^2\cdot \frac{2k}{1/x+1} = 2x &\implies \frac{2k(1+x^3)}{x+1}=2x 
 &\implies k = \frac{x}{x^2-x+1} 
 &\implies d = \frac{2}{x+1}\cdot \frac{x}{x^2-x+1} = \frac{2x}{x^3+1},
 &\overline{d} = \frac{2x^2}{x^3+1}.
\end{align*}
Now we have to locate  $E$ . Since  $DE \perp AC$ ,  $\tfrac{e-d}{d-x} \in i\mathbb{R}$ . And since  $E\in AI$ , the real axis,  $e = \overline{e}$ . So
\begin{align*}
\frac{e-d}{d-x} = \frac{e - \overline{d}}{\overline{d} - 1/x} &\implies (e-d)(\overline{d} - 1/x) = (e-\overline{d})(d-x) 
&\implies \left(e-\frac{2x}{x^3+1}\right) \left(\frac{2x^2}{x^3+1} -\frac{1}{x} \right) = -\left(e-\frac{2x^2}{x^3+1}\right)\left(\frac{2x}{x^3+1} - x\right) 
&\implies e=\frac{2x(x^2+x+1)}{(x^3+1)(x+1)}
\end{align*}
after solving. The reflection of  $I$  over  $AC$  is simply  $2x$ . To finish, we aim to show that the following is real:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\frac{e-b}{b-d}}{\frac{e-2x}{2x-d}} &= \frac{(e-b)(2x-d)}{(b-d)(e-2x)} 
&= \frac{\left(\frac{2x(x^2+x+1)}{(x^3+1)(x+1)} - \frac{2}{x+1}\right) \left( 2x - \frac{2x}{x^3+1}\right)}{\left(\frac{2}{x+1} - \frac{2x}{x^3+1}\right) \left( \frac{2x(x^2+x+1)}{(x^3+1)(x+1)} - 2x\right) } 
&= \frac{\big(2x(x^2+x+1)-2(x^3+1)\big) \big(2x(x^3+1)(x+1) - 2x(x+1)\big) }{\big(2(x^3+1)-2x(x+1)\big)\big( 2x(x^2+x+1) - 2x(x^3+1)(x+1)\big)} 
&= \frac{2x^4(x+1)(2x^2+2x-2)}{2x(2x^3-2x^2-2x+2)(x^2+x+1-(x^3+1)(x+1))} 
&= \frac{4x^4(x^2+x-1)(x+1)}{-4x^3(x+1)(x-1)^2(x^2+x-1)} 
&= \frac{-x}{(x-1)^2},
\end{align*} 
which is a real number.</details>

