<blockquote>Claim: For  $p \ge 100$  a prime, we have  $f(p) = p^2$ .</blockquote>
Why  $p>100$ ?

Feels very standard Functional Equation. Even on contest, the hardest part is like completely proving the claim. Here's solution found with **[DebayuRMO](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/user/592699)** and **[proxima1681](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/user/849747)**.

*Solution:* We will show that  $f(x) = x^2$  for all  $x \in \mathbb{N}$  is the only possible working solution. One can check its valid and now we show that this is one of its kind. Denote  $P(m,n)$  as the assertion to the Functional Equation.

Consider  $P(1,1)$  to get  $f(1) = 1$ . From  $P(m,1)$  and  $P(m,m)$  we get that 
    \begin{align}
        f(m) -m +1 \mid mf(m) + 1 &\implies f(m) -m +1 \mid m^2 -m + 1 \tag{1.1}
        2f(m) + m^2 \mid 2mf(m) &\implies 2f(m) - m^2 \mid m^3 \tag{1.2}
    \end{align}**<span style="color:red">Claim:**</span>  $f(p) = p^2$  for large enough prime  $p$ .

*Proof:* Putting  $m = p$  in  $(1.2)$  we get  $2f(p) - p^2 \mid p^3$ . We have a lot of cases to consider. It is clear that 
        \[2f(p) - p^2 \in \{\pm 1, \pm p, \pm p^2, \pm p^3\}.\]
Observe that  $(1.2)$  gives the bound  $f(m) \le m^2$ . This would dismiss  $\pm p^3$  case since we can take  $p$  large enough to overtake a quadratic term with a cubic term. Observe that for the  $\pm p^2$  case, we have one nice solution and the other one can be rejected by a quick calculation. The final part of the case-work can be done by putting every remaining possibility in  $(1.1)$  and we would be done. $\square$ -----------
To finish off,  $P(p,m)$  with  $f(p) = p^2$  gives 
    \begin{align*}
        p^2-pm+f(m) &\mid p^3-p^2n+pn^2 -p(p^2-pm+f(m))
        p^2-pm+f(m) &\mid p(f(m)-m^2).
    \end{align*}
Fixing  $m$  and taking  $p$  to sufficiently large, we are done.  $\blacksquare$

Setting  $m=n=1$ , we have  $2f(1) - 1 \mid 1$ , or  $f(1) = 1$ . Setting  $m=n$  and  $m=1$  respectively, we have the two relations
\begin{align*}
2f(m) - m^2 &\mid m^3 
1+f(n) - n &\mid n^2-n-1.
\end{align*}
Setting  $m=p$  in the first relation yields   $$ f(p) \in \left\{\frac{p^2+p^3}2, p^2, \frac{p^2+p}2, \frac{p^2+1}2\right\}. $$   On the other hand,  $f(p) \leq p^2$  by the second relation. Now, for sufficiently large  $p$ , 
\begin{align*}
\frac 12(p^2-p+2) &\nmid p^2-p-1 
\frac 12(p^2-2p+3) &\nmid p^2-p-1
\end{align*}
which eliminates all options but  $f(p) = p^2$ .

Now we can extend to all  $n$ . Note that we have   $$ p^2+f(n) - np \mid p^3 + nf(n), $$   so we can in fact write   $$ p^2+f(n) - np \mid p^3+nf(n) - (p+n)(p^2+f(n)-np) = p(f(n) - n^2). $$   By picking sufficiently large  $p$ , the LHS is relatively prime to  $p$ , and furthermore it exceeds  $f(n) - n^2$ . This shows  $f(n) = n^2$ .

We claim the answer is  $f(x)=x^2$ .

Setting  $m=n=1$  implies  $f(1)=1$ . Consider a prime  $p$ , then setting  $m=n=p$  then \begin{align*} 2f(p)-p^2 \mid 2pf(p) &\stackrel{\text{EA}}\iff 2f(p)-p^2 \mid p^3  \implies 2f(p)-p^2 &\in \{\pm 1, \pm p, \pm p^2, \pm p^3 \} \end{align*}
Manually checking each of these cases <details><summary>relatively</summary>for those with a bit of experience</details> <details><summary>routine</summary>I'm too lazy to write this part up  :rotfl:</details> <details><summary>testing</summary>each case gives finitely many possible  $p$ , once finitely many found considering  $m$  in the finite set and  $n$  a prime not in the finite set gives us our desired contradiction for each case</details> gives that  $f(p)=p^2$  for prime  $p$ . Now consider  $m$  and a sufficiently large prime  $p$  with  $f(m)<p$ . Then  $p^2+f(m)-pm \mid p^3+mf(m) \stackrel{\text{EA}}\iff p^2+f(m)-pm \mid p^3-mp^2+pm^2$ . However   $f(m)<p$  implies that the LHS is not divisible by  $p$  so we require  $p^2+f(m)-pm \mid p^2-mp+m^2 \stackrel{\text{EA}}\iff p^2+f(m)-pm \mid f(m)-m^2$ . However as  $p$  grows the LHS grows without bound while the RHS is fixed, therefore the RHS is  $0$  or  $f(m)=m^2$  for all  $m$ , and we are finished.  $\square$

