<details><summary>Solution - grinding ISL problems</summary>$$ \color{red} \spadesuit\color{red} \boxed{\textbf{Initial Assertions.}}\color{red} \spadesuit $$  We begin with some relatively easy steps. Let  $P(m,n)$  denote assertion into   $$ f(m)+f(n)-mn \mid mf(m)-nf(n). $$  We have:  $$ \begin{aligned}
P(1,1) \implies & 2f(1) -1 \mid 2f(1) \implies \boxed{\color{magenta} f(1)=1}
P(m,m)\implies & 2f(m)-m^2 \mid 2mf(m). 
\end{aligned} $$  In the last line, let  $m=p$  be an arbitrary prime number (say, sufficiently large). Then we have  $$ 2f(p)-p^2 \mid 2pf(p); \; \color{green} 2f(p) -p^2 \color{black} \mid 2pf(p)- p(\color{green}2f(p)-p^2\color{black} )=p^3. \quad \quad (*) $$  Thus, we have limited  $f(p)$ 's values to a finite number. But can we do even better?  $$ \color{blue} \clubsuit\color{blue} \boxed{\textbf{Not so much casework.}}\color{blue} \clubsuit $$  Notice that  $P(1;p)$  and the fact that  $\color{magenta} f(1)=1$  imply that  $$ f(p)+1-p \mid pf(p)+1. $$  Applying the magic trick from  $(*)$  again (subtracting a multiple of the divisor so we get rid of awkward expressions), we get that  $$ \color{green} f(p)+1-p \color{black} \mid pf(p)+1-p(\color{green} f(p)+1-p\color{black} )=p^2-p+1. $$  Thus,  $f(p) \le p^2$ . Combining with  $2f(p)-p^2 \mid p^3$ , we have the following  $4$  options for  $f$ :  $$ f(p)=p^2; \; \frac{p^2-p}{2}; \; \frac{p^2 \pm 1}{2}. $$  We test each one individually:  $$ \begin{aligned} & f(p)=p^2: f(p)+1-p=p^2-p+1 \Rightarrow \text { happiness!!! }  & f(p)=\frac{p^2-p}{2}: f(p)+1-p=\frac{(p-1)(p-2)}{2} \nmid p^2-p+1 .  & f(p)=\frac{p^2+1}{2}: f(p)+1-p=\frac{p^2-2 p+3}{2} \nmid p^2-p+1 .  & f(p)=\frac{p^2-1}{2}: f(p)+1-p=\frac{(p-1)^2}{2} \nmid p^2-p+1 .\end{aligned} $$  Thus, for sufficiently large  $p$ , we may assume  $f(p)=p^2$ .  $$ \color{magenta} \bigstar\color{magenta} \boxed{\textbf{Finishing touches.}}\color{magenta} \bigstar $$  From here, simply notice that  $P(p,n)$  yields  $$ p^2+f(n)-pn \mid p^2-np+n^2. $$  We apply the magic trick from  $(*)$  for the third time to obtain that  $$ \color{green} p^2+f(n)-pn\color{black}  \mid p^2-np+n^2-(\color{green} p^2+f(n)-pn\color{black} )=n^2-f(n) $$  Thus,  $p^2+f(n)-pn$  divides  $n^2-f(n)$  for any fixed  $n$  and an arbitrarily large prime number  $p$ . This implies, however, that we can find arbitrarily large divisors of  $n^2-f(n)$  just by making  $p$  larger, and there exist infinitely many prime numbers; thus,  $n^2-f(n)=0$  for all  $n$ , from where we find that  $$ \color{green} \boxed{\therefore f(x)=x^2 \textbf{ is the only solution.}} $$</details>

