\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
&\iff x^9(y^2+z^2)+x^8(y^3-y^2z-y^2z+z^3)+x^7(y^2-z^2)^2+x^6(y^5+z^5-y^3z^2-y^2z^3)-2x^5(y^2+z^2)y^2z^2&+x^4(y^6z+yz^6-y^4z^3-y^3z^4)+x^3(y^6z^2+y^2z^6-2y^4z^4+x^2(y^3z^6+y^6z^3-y^5z^4-y^4z^5)+x(y^6z^4+y^4z^6)=0
\end{align*} $\iff x(x^2(y^2+z^2)-2x(y^2z+yz^2)+y^3z+yz^3)(x^6+x^5(y+z)+(y^2+yz+z^2)x^4+x^3(y+z)(y^2+z^2)+(y^2+z^2+yz)yzx^2+(y+z)y^2z^2x+y^3z^3=0$  $\iff \boxed{x(x^2(y^2+z^2)-2x(y^2z+yz^2)+y^3z+yz^3)(x+y)(x+z)(x^2+y^2)(x^2+z^2)=0}$ It suffices that  $x=0,x+y=0,x+z=0,x^2+y^2=0,x^2+z^2=0$  all cannot happen and if  $x^2=yz$ , then  $(PQXY)$  is cyclic for the problem to be true
If  $x=-y, A=B$ , clearly impossible
If  $x^2+y^2=0$ , then  $A=-B$  implying  $\triangle ABC$  is a right-angle triangle but it is acute, contradiction
Lastly if  $x^2=yz$ , then  $\triangle ABC$  is isoceles which gives  $X=Y$  is the midpoint of  $BC$  so  $PQXY$  is clearly cyclic. Done

