We work in the above configuration to avoid the use of directed angles. The proof can clearly be modified for any configuration.

Define  $P=H_2G_2\cap H_3G_3$  to be the anti-Steiner point of  $HG$ . Note that the angle between  $H_3G_3$  and  $H_2G_2$  is  $2A$  since they are reflections of a common line over two lines with angle  $A$  between them. In our configuration, this means that  $\angle G_3PG_2=\angle H_3PH_2=\pi-2A$ . However, we also have
\[\angle G_3AG_2=\angle G_3AG+\angle G_2AG=2A,\]
and similarly  $\angle H_3AH_2=2A$ . Thus,  $P=(AG_2G_3)\cap(AH_2H_3)=(AG_2G_3)\cap(ABC)$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$ The lemma now destroys the problem as all the circles now concur at  $P$ , the anti-Steiner point of the Euler line.</details>

