We let  $y = 1-b_1$ . Note that using the fractional binomial theorem, we have the closed form 
\begin{align*}
b_n = b_1^{k^{n-1}} &= (1-y)^{k^{n-1}} 
&= 1-k^{n-1}y + \frac{k^{n-1}\left(k^{n-1}-1\right)}2 y^2 - \frac{k^{n-1}\left(k^{n-1}-1\right)\left(k^{n-1}-2\right)}6 y^3 + \cdots 
&> 1-k^{n-1}\left(y+y^2+y^3+\cdots\right) 
&= 1 - \frac{k^{n-1}y}{1-y}.
\end{align*}
Here, the inequality in the penultimate line follows because each  $(-1)^i\tbinom{k^{n-1}}i$  term is negative and has magnitude strictly less than  $k^{n-1}$ . On the other hand, we can determine the closed form  $a_n = 1- \left(\tfrac 12\right)^{n-1} x$  where  $x = 1-a_1$ . As a result, we only require the inequality \[k^{n-1}\left(\frac y{1-y}\right) \leq \left(\frac 12\right)^{n-1} x \iff (2k)^{n-1} \leq \frac{x(1-y)}y\] which is clearly true for sufficiently large  $n$  as  $2k < 1$ .

