	  $$ \int_{0}^{1}f(nx)\, dx=\frac{1}{n}\int_{0}^{n}f(t)\, dt. $$  	Now there are two ways to compute the above limit, one is by using L'Hôpital's rule and the other is through Stolz–Cesàro theorem. We shall see the latter in our solution.
	\begin{align*}
	\lim\cfrac{1}{n}\int_{0}^{n}f(t)\, dt &=\lim\int_{n}^{n+1}f(t)\, dt.
	\end{align*}
	We claim that  $\displaystyle\int_{n}^{n+1}f(t)\, dt\to L$ . Consider a sufficeintly large  $n_{\varepsilon}$  for some  $\varepsilon>0$  such that  $|f(n_{\varepsilon})-L|<\varepsilon$  then it follows that
	\begin{align*}
		\int_{n_{\varepsilon}}^{n_{\varepsilon}+1}f(t)\, dt-L&=\int_{n_\varepsilon}^{n_{\varepsilon}+1}f(t)-L\, dt
		&\le\int_{n_{\varepsilon}}^{n_{\varepsilon}+1}|f(t)-L|\, dt
		&\le\varepsilon.
	\end{align*}

