I see very few nicely worded, formal solutions. TBH, the problem itself is fun and not awfully easy for P1, in my opinion, contrary to what many people suggest here. The main difficulty is to formalise the weirdly decreasing nature of a sequence till it reaches small values.

<blockquote>For each integer  $a_0 > 1$ , define the sequence  $a_0, a_1, a_2, \ldots$  for  $n \geq 0$  as  $$ a_{n+1} = 
\begin{cases}
\sqrt{a_n} & \text{if } \sqrt{a_n} \text{ is an integer,} 
a_n + 3 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases} $$  Determine all values of  $a_0$  such that there exists a number  $A$  such that  $a_n = A$  for infinitely many values of  $n$ .</blockquote>**Answer:** All  $a_0=3n$  for  $n \geqslant 1$  work and these are the only possible numbers which satisfy our condition.

Call a sequence *cute* if it is defined recursively by the given conditions and satisfies  $a_n=A$  for infinitely many  $n$  and some constant  $A$ . 

Notice that if for any  $i$ , if  $a_i \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$  then  $a_{i+k}=a_i+3k$  for all  $k>0$ ; since no perfect square is  $2 \pmod{3}$ . Also,  $3 \mid a_0 \iff 3 \mid a_k$  for all  $k \geqslant 1$ .**Case 1.**  $3 \mid a_0$ .

We show that  $A=3$  works. To see why, let  $k_1, k_2, \dots,$  be the indices where the sequence takes square values, namely  $(3\ell_1)^2, (3\ell_2)^2, \dots,$  respectively. Notice that  $a_{k_i+1}=3\ell_i$  and  $\ell_{i+1}$  is the least positive integer such that  $(3\ell_{i+1})^2 \geqslant (3\ell_i)$ . Consequently,  $\ell_{i+1} \leqslant \ell_i$  with equality only if  $\ell_i=1$  for all  $i>0$ . Hence, there exists an index  $m$  for which  $a_m=9$ ; so the sequence becomes periodic,  $9 \rightarrow 3 \rightarrow 6 \rightarrow 9\rightarrow \dots,$  done!  $\blacksquare$ **Case 2.**  $a_0 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ .

If any term is  $2 \pmod{3}$  we are done. As earlier, define  $k_1, k_2, \dots,$  and consider  $a_{k_i}=(3\ell_i+1)^2$ . Apply previous idea, to see that  $\ell_{i+1}$  is the least positive integer such that  $(3\ell_{i+1}+1)^2 \geqslant (3\ell_i+1)$ . Hence,  $\ell_{i+1} \leqslant \ell_i$  for each  $i>0$  with equality only if  $\ell_i=1$ . Thus, we arrive at  $m$  such that  $a_m=4$  so  $a_{m+1}=2,$  a contradiction!  $\blacksquare$ Our case analysis validates the initial claim; hence  $a_0$  determines a cute sequence if and only if  $a_0 \equiv 0 \pmod{3}$ .

