We have  $RS=\frac{1}{2}RT=\frac{k+1}{2}a$ . Thus  $VS=\frac{k+1}{2}a-a=\frac{k-1}{2}a$ . As  $VJ\cdot VK=VS\cdot VR$ , we obtain  $VJ=\frac{k-1}{2}\cdot\frac{a^2}{b}$ .
The condition we aim to prove is equivalent to  $KT^2=KJ\cdot KA$ . Co-sine law on triangle  $KVT$  yields  $KT^2=(ka)^2+b^2-2kab\cos(\angle{KVT})=(ka)^2+b^2+2kab\cos(\angle{RVK})=k^2a^2+b^2+2kab\cdot\frac{a^2+b^2-c^2}{2ab}=k^2a^2+b^2+k(a^2+b^2-c^2)=k(k+1)a^2+(k+1)b^2-kc^2$ . On the other hand,  $KJ\cdot KA=\left(b+\frac{k-1}{2}\cdot\frac{a^2}{b}\right)(k+1)b=(k+1)b^2+\frac{k^2-1}{2}a^2$ . Equating the two yields  $$ k(k+1)a^2-kc^2=\frac{k^2-1}{2}a^2 $$  so this is what we must prove.

In order to do that, observe that, since the line through  $A$  and  $R$  is tangent to  $\Omega$ , we have  $AR^2=AJ\cdot KA$ ; in the same way as before, we compute  $AR^2=(k+1)a^2+k(k+1)b^2-kc^2$ . We also have  $AJ\cdot KA=\left(kb-\frac{k-1}{2}\cdot\frac{a^2}{b}\right)(k+1)b=k(k+1)b-\frac{k^2-1}{2}a^2$ . Equating the two yields  $$ (k+1)a^2-kc^2=-\frac{k^2-1}{2}a^2 $$  and adding  $(k^2-1)a^2$  to both sides gives the desired equality.

