The key idea is to set  $(AXY)$  to the unit circle. In this frame of reference,  $H$  is the foot from  $A$  to  $XY$ ,  $E$  and  $F$  are the feet from  $H$  to  $AX$  and  $AY$  respectively. Also, by homothety,  $AO$  is simply  $AO'$  where  $O'$  is the center of  $(AXY)$ . We now observe
\[h = \frac{1}{2}(a+x+y-xy/a).\]
We have  $p=ka$  for real  $k$ , and  $HP\perp AY$ , so
\begin{align*}
\frac{h-p}{a-y} = -\frac{\bar{h}-\bar{p}}{1/a-1/y} &\implies h-p = ay(\bar{h}-\bar{p}) 
&\implies h-p = ay\bar{h} - \frac{y}{a}p 
&\implies p(y/a-1) = \frac{1}{2}(y+ay/x+a-a^2/x)-\frac{1}{2}(a+x+y-xy/a) 
&\implies p = \frac{1}{2}(a^2/x+x).
\end{align*}
We see  $\ell=(x+y)/2$ , so  $|p-\ell|=\frac{1}{2}|a^2/x-y|=\frac{1}{2}|a^2-xy|$ . Also,  $|h-\ell|=\frac{1}{2}|a-xy/a|=\frac{1}{2}|a^2-xy|=|p-\ell|$ . We can similarly show that  $|h-\ell|=|p-\ell|=|q-\ell|$ , so  $L$  is the center of  $(HPQ)$ , as desired.

