Let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{BC}$ , and WLOG assume  $AB < AC$ . We claim that the circumcenter  $R$  lies on  $\overline{AM}$ . **Claim:**  $\triangle ABC \sim \triangle HPQ$  and  $(PQH)$  is tangent to  $\overline{AH}$ . 

*Proof.* This is just angle chasing:   $$ \angle HPQ = \angle EPA = 90^\circ - \angle EAP = 90^\circ - \angle DAB = \angle ABC = \angle CHD. $$   Analogously  $\angle HQP = \angle ACB$  and we are done.  $\square$ It follows that  $\overline{HR} \perp \overline{AD}$ , so it suffices to show that  $\triangle AHR \sim \triangle ADM$ . By Power of a Point, we know that   $$ AP \cdot AQ = AP(AP+PQ)= AH^2 \implies PQ = \dfrac{AH^2-AP^2}{AP}. $$   By Extended Law of Sines,   $$ HR = \dfrac{PQ}{2\sin \angle PHQ} = \dfrac{PQ}{2\sin A} = \dfrac{AH^2-AP^2}{2AP \sin A}. $$   Observe that from  $\triangle APB \sim \triangle AHC$ , we have   $$ \dfrac{AP}{AB} = \dfrac{AH}{AC} \implies AH = \dfrac{b}{c} AP, $$   so we can calculate   $$ \dfrac{HR}{AH} = \dfrac{AH^2-AP^2}{2AP \cdot AH \sin A} = \dfrac{(\tfrac{b}{c}AP)^2-AP^2}{2AP(\tfrac{b}{c}AP)\sin A} = \dfrac{b^2-c^2}{2bc\sin A}. $$   We also have   $$ \dfrac{DM}{AD} = \dfrac{\tfrac{a}{2}-c \cos B}{AD} = \dfrac{a^2-2ac \cos B}{2a \cdot AD}. $$   These don't look equal at first glance, but in fact   $$ a^2-2ac \cos B = b^2-c^2 $$   by Law of Cosines and   $$ 2a \cdot AD = 2bc \sin A = 4[ABC] $$   by middle school geometry formulas, so we can conclude that   $$ \dfrac{HR}{AH} = \dfrac{DM}{AD} \implies \triangle AHR \sim \triangle ADM $$   as desired.  $\blacksquare$

