Let  $E'$  and  $F'$  be the reflections of  $H$  across  $E$  and  $F$ , respectively. By orthocenter reflections,  $E'$  and  $F'$  lie on  $(ABC)$ . We claim that  $AQHE'$  and  $APHF'$  are cyclic. Indeed, we have
\begin{align*}
	\angle AQH &= 180^\circ - \angle AQF 
	&= 180^\circ - (90^\circ - \angle QAF) 
	&= 90^\circ + \angle QAF 
	&= 90^\circ + \angle OAB 
	&= 90^\circ + (90^\circ - \angle ACB) 
	&= 180^\circ - \angle ACB 
	&= 180^\circ - \angle AE'B 
	&= 180^\circ - \angle AE'H.
\end{align*}
Hence,  $AQHE'$  is cyclic. Similarly,  $APHF'$  is also cyclic. 

Now, let  $U = (AHQE') \cap \overline{AC} \neq A$ , and let  $V = (AHPF') \cap \overline{AB} \neq A$ . We claim that  $\overline{AU}$  is a diameter of  $(AQHE')$ , and that  $\overline{AV}$  is a diameter of  $(AHPF')$ . Indeed, the center of  $(AHQE')$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{HE'}$ , which is  $\overline{AU}$ . Hence,  $\overline{AU}$  is a diameter of  $(AQHE')$ , and similarly  $\overline{AV}$  is a diameter of  $(AHPF')$ .

Additionally, we claim that  $\overline{AH}$  is tangent to  $(PQH)$ . Indeed, we have
\begin{align*}
	\angle HPQ &= \angle EPA 
	&= 90^\circ - \angle PAE 
	&= 90^\circ - \angle OAC 
	&= 90^\circ - (90^\circ - \angle ABC) 
	&= \angle ABC 
	&= 90^\circ - (90^\circ - \angle ABC) 
	&= 90^\circ - \angle BAH 
	&= \angle AHF 
	&= \angle AHQ,
\end{align*}
which is enough by angle-by-tangent. 

Now, let  $K$  be the circumcenter of  $(PQH)$ , and let  $L = (AU) \cap \overline{AB} \neq A$ . We claim that  $U, H, K, V$  are collinear. Indeed, we have  $\angle AHK = 90^\circ$  (since  $\overline{AH}$  is tangent to  $(PQH)$ ), and  $\angle AHU = \angle AHV = 90^\circ$  (since  $H$  lies on  $(AU)$  and  $(AV)$ ). In addition,  $\overline{UV} \parallel \overline{BC}$ , as both are parallel to  $\overline{AH}$ . Also, since both  $\overline{UL}$  and  $\overline{HQ}$  are perpendicular to  $\overline{AB}$ , we have  $\overline{UL} \parallel \overline{HQ}$ . Since  $LQHU$  is cyclic, this implies that it is an isosceles trapezoid. In particular,  $\overline{QH}$  and  $\overline{LU}$  share the same perpendicular bisector. Therefore, since  $K$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{QH}$ , it also lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{LU}$ . Therefore, as  $\triangle ULV$  is a right triangle, it follows that  $K$  is the circumcenter of  $\triangle ULV$  (it lies on the hypotenuse and the perpendicular bisector of one of the legs), and is in particular the midpoint of  $\overline{UV}$ . Since  $\overline{UV} \parallel \overline{BC}$ , it follows that  $\overline{AK}$  passes through the midpoint of  $\overline{BC}$  as well. Thus,  $K$  lies on the  $A$ -median of  $\triangle ABC$ , as desired.  $\Box$

