This is equivalent to having  $DE = 2 MD \sin (C)$ . WLOG  $AB<AC$  and note that
\begin{align*}
MD &= \frac{1}{2} (CD-BD) = \frac{AD}{2} \left( \frac{\cos(C)}{\sin(C)} - \frac{\cos(B)}{\sin(B)} \right) 
&= \frac{AD}{2} \cdot \frac{\sin(B-C)}{\sin(B)\sin(C)} 
\end{align*}
so  $2MD \sin(C) = AD \cdot \frac{\sin(B-C)}{\sin(B)} = DE$  by law of sines as desired.

We start with what is probably a well-known lemma. However, I did not know it before, so I include it for completeness.**Lemma.** Let  $ABC$  be a triangle with circumcircle  $\Gamma$  and circumcentre  $O$ . Let  $P$  be a point and consider an inversion  $\Psi$  with centre  $P$ . Then the circumcentre of  $\Psi(\triangle ABC)$  lies on line  $OP$ .
*Proof.* Let  $OP$  meet  $\Gamma$  at  $U$  and  $V$ , with  $U$  closer to  $P$ . Then  $U$  is the closest point to  $P$  on  $\Gamma$  and  $V$  is the farthest point. Therefore  $\Psi(U)$  is the farthest point on the  $\Psi(\Gamma)$  from  $P$  and  $\Psi(U)$  is the closest point. It follows that segment  $\overline{\Psi(U)\Psi(V)}$  is a diameter of  $\Psi(\Gamma)$ . Since  $P, O, U, V, \Psi(U), \Psi(V)$  are collinear, it follows  $PO$  passes through the centre of  $\Psi(\Gamma)$ .
