[/asy]**Claim 1.** The lines  $BQ'$  and  $CP'$  are tangent to the circumcircle of  $ABC$ . 
*Proof.* It suffices to show that  $BC$  is tangent to the circumcircle of  $\triangle BQC$ . This is angle chasing. Note that 
\[\measuredangle QCB = 90^\circ - \angle CBF = 90^\circ - \angle CBA \]
and
\[ \measuredangle QAC = \measuredangle BAH = 90^\circ - \angle CBA . \]
Thus  $(BQC)$  is tangent to  $BC$ , so after inversion  $BQ'$  is tangent to  $(ABC)$ . By symmetry,  $CP'$  is also tangent. 
------**Claim 2.** The lines  $BQ'$  and  $CP'$  intersect at the circumcircle of  $\triangle P'Q'H'$ . 
*Proof.* Let  $X$  be the circumcentre of  $P'Q'H'$ . It suffices to show that  $B, Q', X$  are collinear. First, we compute  $\measuredangle BQ'H'$ . We have that  $H'Q' \parallel HQ = CF \parallel BF'$ . Thus 
\[ \measuredangle BQ'H' = \measuredangle Q'B F' = \measuredangle Q'BA - \measuredangle ABF' = \measuredangle BCA - 90^\circ \]
On the other hand, since  $P'H' \parallel PH$ ,
\[ \measuredangle XQ'H' = 90^\circ - \measuredangle H'P'Q' = 90^\circ - \measuredangle PHQ' = 90^\circ - \measuredangle EHA = \measuredangle 90^\circ - \measuredangle ACB .\]
Thus  $\measuredangle BQ'H' = \measuredangle XQ'H'$ . Hence  $B, Q', X$  are collinear, any by symmetry,  $C, P', X$  are as well.
