Let  $D, E,$  and  $F$  denote the feet of the altitudes from  $A, B,$  and  $C$  in  $\triangle{ABC}.$  First, we prove the following lemma: **<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  $\triangle{HPQ} \sim \triangle{ABC}.$  
*Proof.* Firstly, we angle-chase to get
\begin{align*}
    \angle{PHQ} &= \angle{EHC} 
    &= 90^\circ-\angle{ECH} 
    &= 90^\circ-\angle{ACF} 
    &= \angle{BAC}.
\end{align*}
Then, angle-chasing again, we get
\begin{align*}
    \angle{PQH} &= \angle{AQF} 
    &= 90^\circ-\angle{FAQ} 
    &= 90^\circ - \angle{BAC}+\angle{QAC} 
    &= 90^\circ - \angle{BAC} + \angle{OAC} 
    &= 90^\circ - \angle{BAC} + 90^\circ-\angle{ABC} 
    &= 180^\circ - \angle{ABC} - \angle{BAC} 
    &= \angle{ACB}.
\end{align*}
Hence, by the AA similarity criterion, we have proven the claimed result.  $\blacksquare$  

Additionally, observe that
\begin{align*}
    \angle{AHE} &= 90^\circ - \angle{HAE} 
    &= 90^\circ - \angle{DAC} 
    &= 90^\circ-(90^\circ-\angle{ACD}) 
    &= \angle{ACD} 
    &= \angle{ACB} 
    &= \angle{PQH},
\end{align*}
so we know that  $\overline{HA}$  is tangent to  $(HPQ).$  

Now, let  $O_1$  denote the circumcenter of  $\triangle{HPQ},$  let  $M$  denote the midpoint of  $\overline{PQ},$  and let  $N$  denote the midpoint of  $\overline{BC}.$  

Since  $\overline{HA}$  is tangent to  $(HPQ),$  we know that  $\angle{O_1HA}=90^\circ,$  and since  $O_1$  is the circumcenter of  $(HPQ),$  we know that it lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{PQ},$  so we get
\begin{align*}
    \angle{O_1MA} &= \angle{O_1MP} 
    &= 90^\circ.
\end{align*}
Hence, we know that  $O_1HAM$  is cyclic, so we can angle-chase and use the spiral similarity from  $(HPQ)$  to  $(ABC)$  to get
\begin{align*}
    \angle{OAO_1} &= \angle{MAO_1} 
    &= \angle{MHO_1} 
    &= \angle{NAO} 
    &= \angle{OAN}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, it immediately follows that  $O_1$  lies on  $\overline{AN},$  concluding the proof.  $\square$

