Funny problem.**<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>** For all  $x$  we have  $P(x) < 0$ .
*Proof.* Note that it suffices to show that,
\begin{align*}
\prod (x+a_i) &> M(x+1)^k
\end{align*}
However note that by AM-GM we find,
\begin{align*}
a_i \sqrt[a_i]{x+1} < \left(x+1 + \underbrace{1 + 1 + \dots + 1}_{a_i - 1}\right) = x + a_i
\end{align*}
where equality cannot hold as  $x > 0$ . Thus,
\begin{align*}
\prod (x+a_i) &> \prod \left( a_i \cdot \sqrt[a_i]{x+1} \right)
&= M(x+1)^k
\end{align*}
Thus indeed we find  $P(x) < 0$  for all  $x$  and we are done.  $\square$

Working backwards, we have
\begin{align*}
M(x+1)^k = \prod \left( a_i (x+1)^{\left( \tfrac{1}{a_i} \right)} \right) &< \prod (x+a_i) 
\prod (x+1)^{\left( \tfrac{1}{a_i} \right)} &< \prod \left( \frac{x}{a_i} + 1 \right).
\end{align*}
In fact, each term on the LHS is less than its corresponding term on the RHS:
\begin{align*}
(x+1)^{\left( \tfrac{1}{a_i} \right)} &\leq \left( \frac{x}{a_i} + 1 \right) 
x+1 &\leq \left( \frac{x}{a_i} + 1 \right)^{a_i}
\end{align*}
which is true by Bernoulli's inequality; the equality case will not hold for whichever  $a_i$  is not equal to  $1$ .

