Let  $\ell$  be the length of the tangent from  $M$  to the  $A$ -excircle. By Casey's theorem, it suffices to have 
\[MP\cdot QD+MQ\cdot PD=\ell\cdot PQ.\]
We show that  $MP=MQ$ . It suffices to show that  $M$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $PQ$ , i.e.  $MM_1\perp PQ$  where  $M_1$  is the midpoint of  $AI_A$  and also the center of  $(APFI_AEQ)$ , the circle with diameter  $AI_A$ . Take a homothety at  $A$  with ratio  $2$ . It sends  $M$  to  $D$  and  $M_1$  to  $I_A$ . but  $DI_A$  is trivially perpendicular to  $BC$ . so indeed  $MP=MQ$ .

The equation simplifies to \[MP\cdot PQ=\ell\cdot PQ,\] or \[MP=\ell.\] It suffices to show that  $M$  has equal power to the  $A$ -excircle and the point circle  $(P)$ . Let  $\omega$  be the  $A$ -excircle. By linearity of power of a point, we we have that \[2(\text{Pow}_{\omega}M-MP^2)=(\text{Pow}_{\omega}{A}-AP^2)+(\text{Pow}_{\omega}{D}-DP^2).\] We wish to show that the left hand side equals  $0$ , so it suffices to show that \[(\text{Pow}_{\omega}{A}-AP^2)+(\text{Pow}_{\omega}{D}-DP^2)=0.\]
Note that  $D$  is on  $\omega$ , so  $\text{Pow}_{\omega}{D}=0$ . Also,  $\text{Pow}_{\omega}{A}=FA^2$ . It suffices to show that \[FA^2=PA^2+PD^2.\]This is trivial, since \[AP^2=AI_A^2-I_AP^2=FA^2+I_AF^2-I_AP^2=FA^2+I_AF^2-(DP^2+DI_A^2)=FA^2-DP^2\] from which the conclusion follows. (We just used Pythagorean Theorem multiple times.)

Let  $T=(DEF)\cap AD$ . Let  $N$  be the intersection point of  $AD$  and  $(AEF)$ . Let  $I$  be the center of  $(AEF)$ .
Observe that  $N$  is the midpoint of  $DT$  as  $\measuredangle AHI_A=90^\circ$ . Hence, by PoP,  $$ DN\cdot DA=DP\cdot DQ\implies DM\cdot DT=DP\cdot DQ, $$  therefore  $M$  lies on  $(PTQ)$ .

Note that  $A,I,I_A$  are collinear, hence  $MI\parallel DI_A\perp PQ$ , hence  $MP=MQ$ . Now,  $\measuredangle  QTD=\measuredangle QPM=\measuredangle MQP=\measuredangle  DTP$ .

