We will show that  $(p,q)=(11,17),(17,11)$  are the only solutions. For calculations purposes, do note that  $561=3 \times 11 \times 17$ . We first verify that it indeed works. By **Fermat's Little Theorem**, we have:
\begin{align*}
n^3 \equiv n \pmod{3} \implies n^{33} \equiv n^{11} \equiv n \pmod{3} \implies \boxed{n^{561} \equiv a^{17} \equiv a \pmod{3}} 
n^{11} \equiv n \pmod{11} \implies n^{561} \equiv n^{51} \equiv n \pmod{11} \implies \boxed{n^{561} \equiv a \pmod{11}} 
n^{17} \equiv n \pmod{17} \implies n^{561} \equiv n^{33} \equiv n \pmod{17} \implies \boxed{n^{561} \equiv a \pmod{17}}
\end{align*}
Combining boxed stuff, we see that the claimed solutions work. We now proceed to show that they are the only solutions. Without loss of generality, assume  $p \le q$ .  Now, choose  $n$  such that it is a primitive root modulo  $p$  which clearly exists. Then  $n^{3pq-1} \equiv 1 \pmod{p}$ . This gives the fact that  $p-1 \mid 3pq-1 \iff p-1 \mid 3q-1$ . In a similar fashion, we get  $q-1 \mid 3p-1$ .

Now, we eliminate the possibility of equality between the two primes. Assume that  $p=q$  then from the above divisibility relation, we easily get the fact  $p-1 \mid 3p-1 \iff p-1 \mid 2 \iff q=p=3$ . This would clearly fail, for a counterexample  $2^{27} \not \equiv 2 \pmod{27}$ . So the inequality is strict.

Proceeding, we will now bound the primes with the help of the above divisibility conditions. As  $p \ne q$  then  $p+2 \le q$ . Now rearrange to get:
\[3 \ge \frac{3p+3}{q-1} >\frac{3p-1}{q-1}\]
Now, remember that we said that  $q-1\mid 3p-1$  so the extreme left-hand side is an integer. The only working possibility is that  $\frac{3p-1}{q-1}=2$  as the fraction cannot be equal to  $1$  otherwise we get  $p=1$  which is absurd as  $p$  is a prime. So, simplifying  $\frac{3p-1}{q-1}=2$  we get  $3p+1=2q$ .

We will now finish this Recall that  $p-1 \mid 3q-1 \implies p-1 \mid 6q-2$ . Also,  $3p+1=2q$  gives  $6q-2=9q+1$ . Upon substitution, we get  $p-1 \mid 9p+1 \implies p-1 \mid 10$ . This gives  $p=11$  and resubstituting, we get  $q=17$  which are the claimed solutions.  $\blacksquare$

