A big definition challenge. Here's another way to state the case bash method (the official Solution uses a clever parity orientation, and the Solutions above explicitly checks row-by-row; which I couldn't think of an efficient way to phrase.) 
We will start with some notations to categorize favorable shapes (as I'm baffled as how to deal with complicated concave shapes.)  $\color{green} ule{25cm}{3pt}$   $\color{green} 	extbf{	ext{Tedious notations.}}$  Define a  $	extit{normal polyline}$  to be a non-self intersecting broken line, formed by lattice vertices with coinciding start point and end point. From that, we can define the  $	extit{region}$  of a normal polyline in the usual manner. Let a  $	extit{normal polyline}$  to be  $	extit{rectangular}$  if no two segment  $	extit{face}$  each other when they are oriented outwards the  $	extit{region}$  (i.e. we can shift the lattice segments to form a big rectangle.) Moreover, define the polyline/region to be  $	extit{domino-tileable}$  if they can completely be covered by dominoes. 
Now we consider the position of each domino in relation to its  $	extit{region}$ . Say a region  $\mathcal{R}$  to be  $	extit{composed}$  of dominoes  $D_1,D_2,\ldots,D_k$  if  $D_1 \cup D_2 \ldots \cup D_k = \mathcal{R}$ , and further, let  $\mathcal{R}$  to be formed by the regions  $\mathcal{R}_1, \mathcal{R}_2, \ldots, \mathcal{R}_k = \mathcal{R}$  when
\[ \mathcal{R}_i = D_1 \cup D_2 \ldots \cup D_i \]
We now proceed with the problem.  $\color{blue} ule{25cm}{1pt}$  $\color{blue} 	extbf{	ext{Simplicity of rectangular normal polylines.}}$  A rectangular domino-tibleable normal region can be entirely formed by rectangular normal regions.  $\color{blue} 	extbf{	ext{Proof 1.}}$  It is sufficient to remove one domino  $D$  so that  $\mathcal{R}-D$  is a rectangular normal region; then we can easily proceed by induction. 
We will further claim that the domino we remove has a long side which are connected to a short side which are in the  $	extit{boundary}$  of the polyline, implying the  $\color{blue} 	extbf{	ext{Claim}}$ .
[asy]usepackage("tikz");label(" egin{tikzpicture}
\draw[green,thick] (0,3)--(1,3)--(1,1); \draw[green,thick](1,1)--(4.2,1); \draw[green,thick] (4,1)--(4,-0.8); \draw[green!50!black,very thick](1,2)--(3,2)--(3,1); \draw[red!20!white, thin] (0,3)--(0,1)--(1,1); \draw[red!20!white,thin] (2,1)--(2,0)--(4,0); 
\draw[->](2,2)--(2,3.3); 
ode at (2,3.45) {none};
\draw[->](3,1.5)--(4.1,1.5); 
ode at (4.7,1.5) {none};
\end{tikzpicture}");[/asy]
Consider a domino which has a highest cell, and among those, pick the domino which has the biggest  $x$ -coordinate among the highest cells. Since that domino's entire top side must be part of the boundary, both of its right and left side must coincide with some other domino. Now we consider the domino on its right; if there are two, pick the one above. Again, the top side of that domino must be part of the boundary, and we can always chain this process indefinitely if all the dominoes cannot be freely removed. However, the process must terminate, since there is a rightmost line the dominoes cannot pass.  $ lacksquare$ [asy]usepackage("tikz");label(" egin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.4]
\draw[gray,thin](-2,-2)--(-2,5)--(8,5)--(8,-2)--cycle;
\draw[green,thick](4,5)--(4,3)--(5,3)--(5,5)--cycle; \draw[green,thick](5,2)--(5,4)--(6,4)--(6,2)--cycle; \draw[green,thick](6,1)--(6,3)--(7,3)--(7,1)--cycle; \draw[green,thick](6,3)--(8,3)--(8,4)--(6,4)--cycle; 
\draw[->](4.5,3.5)--(5.3,3.5); \draw[->](5.5,3.5)--(6.9,3.5);
\draw[red,very thick] (4,4)--(4,3)--(5,3)--(5,4);
\end{tikzpicture}");[/asy] $\color{red}ule{25cm}{1pt}$  $\color{red} 	extbf{	ext{Coloring Intentions.}}$  We will devise an algorithm to color the vertices of the dominoes, and proceed to do so inductively along all regions  $\mathcal{R}_1,\mathcal{R}_2,\ldots$  until  $\mathcal{R}_k$ . The process is as following:
[list = disc][*] Color  $\mathcal{R}_1$ 's 6 vertices with  $1,3,1,2,3,2$  in its  $2 	imes 1$  orientation, [*] Whenever a domino is added (to the boundary of the previous region), an uncolored vertex which lies in the corner of the domino follows its long-side mate, and the middle vertex will follow its fellow middle vertex, [*] The boundary's pattern will stay  $``	ext{consistent}"$ : say that it is formed by segments  $s_1,s_2,\ldots, s_l$ , then the colors on  $s_i$  will alternate, and when the boundary transits from  $s_i$  to  $s_{i+1}$ , the color pallete in  $s_{i+1}$  will consist of the transiting vertex and the color which does not appear in  $s_i$ . [/list] $\color{red} 	extbf{	ext{Proof 2.}}$  Good thing this has only  $\sim 5$  cases --- the optimization lies on the fact that  $	extbf{free sides and vertices do exist}$ , and there is less tedious verifications needed when the concavity is deep (i.e. when the domino is covered in a hole, or its 6 vertices is defined predeterminately inside a winding hole --- not to mention if the hole itself is larger than the polygon so there's no easy way to induct it.) 
