There are  $2^{n-1}$  such permutations.
Let  $P(1)=k$ . A valid permutation should look something like this,
\begin{tabular}{l l l l l l l l l}
		{\color{red}  $k$ } & \underline{\hspace{0.3cm}} $\ldots$ \underline{\hspace{0.3cm}} & \textcolor{red}{ $k+1$ } &\underline{\hspace{0.3cm}} $\ldots$ \underline{\hspace{0.3cm}} &\textcolor{red}{ $k+2$ } & \underline{\hspace{0.3cm}} $\ldots$ \underline{\hspace{0.3cm}}  & $\cdots$  &\underline{\hspace{0.3cm}} $\ldots$ \underline{\hspace{0.3cm}} & \textcolor{red}{ $k+n-k$ }.
\end{tabular}
Where the empty spaces can be filled by the set of numbers  $1,2,\ldots, k-1$  in descending order. Then we realise that there is bijection between the ways to fill  $n-k+1$  jars with  $k-1$  marbles and filling the empty spaces with  $k-1$  numbers in descending order. So, we have a formula  considering  $k=1,2,\ldots, n$  given by,   $$ \# \text{permutations}=\sum_{1\le k\le n}\binom{n-k+1+k-1-1}{k-1}=\sum_{1\le k\le n}\binom{n-1}{k-1}=2^{n-1}. $$  This proves our initial claim and we are done.  $\square$

First, note that all but one of the tokens must move to the top row and then back to the bottom, because if there are two tokens that both remain in the bottom row, there is no way of them reversing their order. Thus, we must have  $2\cdot 12=24$  vertical movements. 

Then, note that the total number of horizontal movements must be at least   $$ 12+10+8+6+4+2+0+2+4+6+8+10+12=84 $$  Thus, we need at least  $24+84=\boxed{108}$  movements. We can achieve our goal in  $108$  movements with the following steps:
