We first remove the common factor of  $n$  from everything for convenience. Then  $n^3$  is
\begin{align*}
\frac{A(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)+4(n-1)(n-2)+12(n-1)+D}{24}
\end{align*}
and since there's only  $1$  group of factors (?) that can give a  $n^3$  term, we must have  $A=24$ . Then we expand everything, and the numerator would be
\begin{align*}
A(n^3-6n^2+11n-6) 
+ 4B(n^2-3n+2) 
+ 12C(n-1) 
+ 24D
\end{align*}
and we want everything except that  $An^3$  in front to cancel off. You could set up a system of equations if you really wanted to, but that isn't necessary. Then  $4B-6A=0$ , implying  $B=36$ . Repeat this logic for everything else, to get  $(A, B, C, D)=(24, 36, 14, 1)$ , giving an answer of  $\boxed{75}$ .

