First, some preliminary definitions and measurements: Let  $H$  be the foot of the  $A$ -altitude,  $I_A$  be the  $A$ -excenter,  $D$  be the point that the  $A$ -excircle touches  $\overline{BC},$  and  $M_A,N_A$  be the midpoints of minor and major arc  $\widehat{BC}.$ Further let  $K,s,r,r_A,R$  be the area, semiperimeter, inradius,  $A$ -exradius, and circumradius of  $\triangle ABC.$  

Then  $s=\tfrac{15}{2},K=\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}=\tfrac{15\sqrt7}{4},r=\tfrac{K}{s}=\tfrac{\sqrt7}{2},r_A=\tfrac{K}{s-a}=\tfrac{3\sqrt7}{2},R=\tfrac{abc}{4K}=\tfrac{8}{\sqrt7}.$ We also compute  $AI=\sqrt{r^2+(s-a)^2}=2\sqrt2,AH=\tfrac{2K}{a}=\tfrac{3\sqrt7}{2},BH=\sqrt{AB^2-AH^2}=\tfrac{1}{2},CD=s-b=\tfrac{1}{2},$  the latter three being in preparation to compute  $AD.$  Indeed,  $AD=\sqrt{AH^2+HD^2}=\sqrt{\tfrac{63}{4}+(a-DC-BH)^2}=\tfrac{3\sqrt{11}}{2}.$ We care about  $AD$  because  $\sqrt{bc}$  inversion will yield information regarding lengths involving  $X.$  Indeed,  $\sqrt{bc}$  inversion swaps  $X$  and  $D$  so  $AX=\tfrac{AB\cdot AC}{AD}=\tfrac{16}{\sqrt{11}}.$  Moreover, as  $I$  maps to  $I_A$ , by the inversion distance formula we get  $IX=\tfrac{I_AD\cdot AI\cdot AX}{AB\cdot AC}=\tfrac{r_A\cdot AI\cdot AX}{AB\cdot AC}=2\sqrt{\tfrac{14}{11}}.$  Also,  $\triangle IAX\sim \triangle IN_AM_A\implies \tfrac{IN_A}{2R}=\tfrac{IA}{AX}\implies IN_A=\tfrac{2\sqrt{22}}{\sqrt{7}}.$ Why did we do all of this work? By Poncelet's Porism, we know that  $YZ$  is tangent to  $\omega.$  We already know  $r$  and  $R,$  so if we can nail down some relationship between  $X$  and  $I,$  everything else can be uniquely determined and (hopefully) easily computable. We also computed  $IN_A$  because we want to use the relationship  $\tfrac{xyz}{4R}=[XYZ]=\tfrac{r(x+y+z)}{2},$  and  $\sqrt{bc}$  inversion at  $X$  can give information about  $yz$  in terms of  $XI$  and  $XI_X$  where  $I_X$  is the  $X$ -excenter of  $\triangle XYZ.$ We go on with the computation. Let  $s'$  be the semiperimeter of  $\triangle XYZ.$  We have  $XI_X=XI+2IN_A$  from Fact 5, so  $yz=XI(XI+2IN_A)=\tfrac{232}{11}.$  Thus  $s'=\tfrac{xyz}{2rR}=\tfrac{29x}{22},$  and  $XI^2=r^2+(s'-x)^2\implies (s'-x)^2=\left(\tfrac{7x}{22}\right)^2=\tfrac{147}{44},$  whence we finally have  $x^2=\boxed{33}.$

