From Cauchy inequality we have:   $$  (a+b+c)((2ab+a)+(2bc+b)+(2ca+c)) \ge (\sqrt{a^2(2b+1)}+\sqrt{b^2(2c+1)}+\sqrt{c^2(2a+1)})^2  $$   
On the other hand we can rewrite it as follows: 
\begin{align*} 
\sqrt{1+2ab+2bc+2ca} \ge a\sqrt{2b+1}+b\sqrt{2c+1}+c\sqrt{2a+1}  
\sqrt{1+1-(a^2+b^2+c^2)} \ge a\sqrt{2b+1}+b\sqrt{2c+1}+c\sqrt{2a+1}  
\sqrt{2-(a^2+b^2+c^2)} \ge a\sqrt{2b+1}+b\sqrt{2c+1}+c\sqrt{2a+1}
\end{align*}
which is what we needed.

**Generalization:** Let  $ABC$  be a triangle with circumcircle  $(O)$  and let external  $\angle A$ -bisector intersect  $BC$  at  $S$ . Let  $T$  be a point on polar of  $S$  wrt.  $(O)$ . Let tangents from  $T$  to  $(O)$  intersersect  $BC$  at  $P$  and  $Q$ . Then  $AP$  and  $AQ$  are isogonal wrt.  $\angle A$ .**Proof:** Let  $S'$  be intersection of internal  $\angle A$ -bisector and  $BC$ , let tangents from  $T$  to  $(O)$  touch  $O$  at  $Y$  and  $Z$  and let one of tangents from  $S$  to  $(O)$  touch  $(O)$  at  $X$ . Since  $(B,C;S,S') = -1$   $\implies$   $T$ ,  $S'$  and  $X$  are collinear on polar of  $S$  wrt.  $(O)$ . By La Hire theorem  $S$  lies on  $YZ$ . Since  $(O)$  is incircle/excircle of the triangle formed by  $SX$ ,  $TY$ ,  $TZ$ , we have  $T(P,Q;S,S') = -1$ , which combined with  $AS\perp AS'$  gives that  $AP$  and  $AQ$  are isogonal wrt.  $\angle A$ .

