Let  $D$  be the excircle-touch point to  $\overline{BC}$  and denote by  $K$  the intersection of the external tangents to  $\omega$  and  $\omega_A$ . Also let  $T$  be the  $A$ -mixintillinear touch point and  $\omega_T$  as the  $A$ -mixintillinear circle.**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>**  $\overline{KA}$  is isogonal to  $\overline{AD}$  with respect to  $\angle BAC$ .
*Proof.* As  $\overline{AT}$  is isogonal to  $\overline{AD}$  it suffices to show  $\overline{TAK}$ . Note that by Monge on  $\omega_T$ ,  $\omega$  and  $\omega_A$  we find the points  $T$ ,  $A$  and  $K$  collinear so we are done.  $\square$ **<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>**  $(P, Q)$  is a reciprocal pair under the isogonality involution  $f$ .
*Proof.* Note that,


- It follows from  $3$ -point DDIT on  $\triangle ABC$  and tangent  $\omega_A$  that the following are reciprocal pairs:  $(\overline{KP}, \overline{KQ} )$ ,  $(\overline{KB}, \overline{KC})$  and  $(\overline{KA}, \overline{KD})$ .
- Projecting through  $K$  onto  $\overline{BC}$ , which preserves involution, we find the reciprocal pairs  $(P, Q)$ ,  $(B, C)$  and  $(\overline{KA} \cap \overline{BC}, D)$ .
- Projecting from  $A$  we find the reciprocal pairs  $(\overline{AP}, \overline{AQ})$ ,  $(\overline{AB}, \overline{AC})$  and  $(\overline{AK}, \overline{AD})$

However now from our initial claim we see that the involution with reciprocal pairs  $(\overline{AB}, \overline{AC})$  and  $(\overline{AK}, \overline{AD})$  is simply the isogonality involution. Thus the lines  $\overline{AP}$  and  $\overline{AQ}$  are isogonal.  $\square$ Then it follows that  $\angle BAP = \angle CAQ$  as desired.  $\square$

Very beautiful.

Invert about the excircle with center  $I.$  Now  $BC,(ABC)$  and the two common tangents are sent to congruent circles since they all have radius half that of the excircle. But now  $P,Q$  must be the top points on their circles, since  $PI,QI$  are symmetric to the images of the common tangents and  $I$  is the bottom point of the image of  $BC.$  But  $A$  is the bottom point on the image of  $(ABC),$  by rotation of the image of  $BC$  about the shared midpoint of  $BC$  and  $AD.$  Thus the midpoints  $M,N$  of  $AP,AQ$  are tangency points of the images of the common tangents with the image of  $(ABC).$ We want to show  $\angle IPA=\angle IQA,$  which is equivalent to  $\angle IPM=\angle IQN,$  which is equivalent to arcs  $IM,IN$  being equal, but this is clear by symmetry.

